            <ArticleTitle>Myocardial oxygen pressure across the border zone during acute coronary artery occlusion in the pig heart.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>57-65</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An experimental model for studies of local myocardial ischemia is described. During coronary artery occlusion of various durations, from two to sixty minutes, tissue oxygen pressure (ptO2) was measured across the &quot;border zone&quot; i.e., the tissue between normal and ischemic myocardium. PtO2 was measured with a surface oxygen electrode with eight measuring points arranged in an array. In fourteen pigs a total of forty coronary artery occlusions were studied. During occlusion one single pattern of ptO2 values was found; the measurements from the first point, closest to the core of ischemia, dropped rapidly to zero while the second, third and fourth measuring points showed a stepwise slower decrease in ptO2. In four animals we found a spontaneous increase in ptO2 during coronary artery occlusion. Upon reopening of the occluded artery there was a rapid rise in ptO2 as well as an &quot;overshoot&quot;. Our findings support the hypothesis of a narrow &quot;border zone&quot; with a small residual blood flow thus subjectable to treatment aiming at reduction in the size of the infarct.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
